 Low-speed centrifugation is used to separate large particles and organelles based on their size and density. 

A. Golgi apparatus and endoplasmic reticulum are both large organelles and can be separated by low-speed centrifugation.

B. Lysosomes and endosomes are both membrane-bound organelles, but they are relatively small, so they are unlikely to be separated by low-speed centrifugation.

C. Plasma membrane and cytoskeleton are not organelles, but rather components of the cell structure. They are also relatively small, so they are unlikely to be separated by low-speed centrifugation.

D. DNA and mRNA are both nucleic acids, but they are relatively small, so they are unlikely to be separated by low-speed centrifugation.

E. Proteins and lipids are components of the cell membrane, but they are not organelles, so they are unlikely to be separated by low-speed centrifugation.

F. Nuclei and secretory vesicles are both organelles, but they are relatively small, so they are unlikely to be separated by low-speed centrifugation.

G. DNA and proteins can be separated by low-speed centrifugation if the DNA is bound to a protein, such as histone, in a complex called chromatin. The DNA-protein complex will sediment more quickly than the free DNA and proteins, allowing them to be separated.

So, the two that could be separated by performing low-speed centrifugation are Golgi apparatus and endoplasmic reticulum (A) and DNA and proteins (G).
