 The de Broglie wavelength of a particle is given by the formula:

\[ \lambda = \frac{h}{p} \]

where \( h \) is the Planck constant and \( p \) is the momentum of the particle. The momentum of a particle can be calculated using its mass (\( m \)) and velocity (\( v \)):

\[ p = m \cdot v \]

For the truck, the mass is \( 10,000 \) kg and the velocity is \( 27.8 \) m/s. For the flea, the mass is \( 50 \) mg (which is \( 50 \times 10^{-3} \) kg) and the velocity is \( 1 \) m/s. For the water molecule, the mass is \( 18 \) atomic mass units (approximately \( 2.99 \times 10^{-26} \) kg) and the velocity is \( 500 \) m/s.

Let's calculate the de Broglie wavelengths for each:

1. For the truck:
   \[ \lambda_{\text{truck}} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{10,000 \text{ kg} \cdot 27.8 \text{ m/s}} \]
   \[ \lambda_{\text{truck}} \approx 2.4 \times 10^{-36} \text{ cm} \]

2. For the flea:
   \[ \lambda_{\text{flea}} = \frac{6.626 \times 10^{-34} \text{ J s}}{50 \times 10^{-3} \text{ kg} \cdot 1 \text{ m/s}} \]
   \[ \lambda_{\text{flea}} \approx 1.3 \times 10^{-26} \text{ cm} \]

